{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"a4edc893d2eeb6de9","tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_014bcJbEA2ytTVrNHy4sK9vY","status":"completed","output_file":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-n48Iqv/tmp/claude-31072124/-local-home-scheschb--harvest-work-harvest-work-n48Iqv-translated-rust/6de6788a-c8a8-4554-8fe2-4313825db3b2/tasks/a4edc893d2eeb6de9.output","summary":"All experiments complete. Here is the audit.\n\n# Audit result: 6 concrete divergences\n\n## Areas with NO divergence found\n\n**Area 1 — `c_atoi` vs glibc `atoi`: NO DIVERGENCE.** I compiled a glibc harness (`atoi()` on a hex-decoded, zero-filled buffer) and a Rust harness that `#[path]`-includes `imp.rs` and calls `imp::c_atoi` on the identical bytes. **287,000+ inputs produced byte-identical results**, covering: empty, all-whitespace forms (` \\t\\n\\v\\f\\r`), `\"+\"`/`\"-\"`/`\"++\"`/`\"--\"`/`\"+-\"`/`\" + 5\"`, `0x10`, `0b11`, `1e5`, `1.9`, `1,000`, leading zeros, embedded/leading/trailing NUL, high bytes (`\\xff`, `\\xa0`, UTF-8 and invalid UTF-8), INT_MAX/INT_MIN ±1, 2^32±1 and 2400 multiples of 2^32 (verifying low-32-bit truncation), LONG_MAX/LONG_MIN ±1 and `\"9\"*100` (verifying `strtol` saturation then truncation), the 13-char boundary cases (`9999999999999`, `-999999999999`, `1234567890123`), **all 1- and 2-byte inputs over the full 0..255 byte range**, and **all 3-byte inputs over a 24-symbol alphabet**. The whitespace set and sign/overflow handling are exactly right. Locale is irrelevant (the C never calls `setlocale`; verified `LC_ALL=en_US.iso88591` etc. do not change `atoi(\"\\xa05\")`).\n\n**Area 2 — `Io::fgets` vs `fgets(buf,14,stdin)`: NO DIVERGENCE.** 347 structured cases (line lengths 0..30 with/without newline, CRLF, no-final-newline, NUL at all 16 positions of a 16-byte line, whitespace-only runs, 6-line inputs, exact 12/13/14/15/26/27/28-char lines, 13-char line where the newline is left for the *second* `fgets`) plus a 2,932-case randomized fuzz — all byte-identical in (exit status, stdout). The 13-byte cap, newline retention, NULL-only-on-EOF-before-any-byte, cross-call remainder, and embedded-NUL handling are all correct.\n\n**Area 3 — output formatting: NO DIVERGENCE.** 65,791 byte strings through the exported `printLine` (all 1- and 2-byte non-NUL strings, invalid UTF-8 / surrogates / overlongs, `\"%s %d %n\"`, `\"\"`, a 200 KB string) and 609 values through `printIntLine` (all of −300..300 plus INT_MIN/INT_MAX) are byte-identical to the C `.so`. `INT_MIN` prints `-2147483648` in both.\n\n**Area 4 — control flow / ordering: NO DIVERGENCE.** Nominal run (`2\\n5\\n`) is byte-identical; `goodG2B` before `goodB2G`, `good()` before `bad()`, and all five `printLine` positions match.\n\n**Executable-level OOB sweep: NO DIVERGENCE.** Indices 0..200 for both the `bad` sink and the `goodB2G` sink, 20 reps each, stdout to a pipe: identical (crash exactly at {16,17,18,19,26,27}). As instructed, I am not reporting this area.\n\n---\n\n## Divergence 1 — SIGPIPE: C dies, Rust exits 0\n\n(a) **Input:** stdin `\"7\\n3\\n\"`; stdout = a pipe whose read end is already closed.\n(b) **C:** killed by `SIGPIPE`, wait status signal 13 (shell `$?` = 141), no output. **30/30 runs.**\n(c) **Rust:** exits **0**, no output. **30/30 runs.**\n(d) Rust's `std` runtime sets `SIGPIPE` to `SIG_IGN` before `main`, and every write is error-swallowed: `imp.rs:53` (`let _ = self.stdout.flush()`), `imp.rs:101`, `imp.rs:107-108`, `imp.rs:113` (all `let _ = write!/write_all`). Nothing in `main.rs:14-19` restores the default disposition.\nNote: `driver | head -1` does *not* expose this (head consumes all 142 bytes), so a naive pipeline test misses it.\n\n## Divergence 2 — stdout is not line-buffered when stdout is a terminal\n\n(a) **Input:** stdin `\"-1\\n16\\n\"`, **stdout attached to a pty**.\n(b) **C:** stdout is line-buffered on a tty, so every line is flushed as written; 167 bytes appear (`Calling good()...` through `Finished bad()`), then `SIGSEGV`. **25/25 runs, deterministic.**\n(c) **Rust:** 0 bytes, then `SIGSEGV`. **25/25 runs.**\n(d) `imp.rs:41` / `imp.rs:48`: `stdout: BufWriter::new(io::stdout())` is unconditionally block-buffered; there is no isatty check.\n\nFull set of tty-stdout divergent indices in 0..200 (each 25/25 reproducible): **k=16, 17, 26, 27 → C emits 167 bytes; k=18, 19 → C emits 151 bytes; Rust emits 0 in all six.** (C's 151-vs-167 split is itself information Rust cannot reproduce: k=18/19 corrupt `bad()`'s return address so C dies *after* the ten zeros but *before* `Finished bad()`.) Large indices likewise: `-1\\n1000000\\n` → C emits 121 bytes, Rust 0.\n\nSecond manifestation, no crash needed — interactive transcript (stdin *and* stdout on the same pty, user types `3` then `5`):\n```\nC : Calling good()...|0|...|1|0|0|<<<TYPE 3>>>3|0|...|Finished good()|Calling bad()...|<<<TYPE 5>>>5|0|...|Finished bad()|\nRS: <<<TYPE 3>>>3|<<<TYPE 5>>>5|Calling good()...|0|...|Finished bad()|\n```\n\n## Divergence 3 — seekable stdin is left at EOF instead of the logical read position\n\n(a) **Input:** a regular file `7\\nrest-line-1\\nAAA\\nBBB\\n` (22 bytes) redirected as stdin; run `{ driver >/dev/null; cat; } < file`.\n(b) **C:** glibc repositions seekable input streams at exit; the shared fd offset ends at **14** (exactly `\"7\\n\"` + `\"rest-line-1\\n\"`), so the next reader sees `AAA\\nBBB\\n`. **10/10 runs**, confirmed by `lseek(fd,0,SEEK_CUR)` after the child exits.\n(c) **Rust:** fd offset ends at **22** (EOF); the next reader sees nothing.\n(d) `imp.rs:40` + `imp.rs:57-67`: reads go through Rust's globally buffered `io::Stdin` (8 KiB `BufReader`), which slurps the whole file, and `main.rs:14-19` only flushes stdout — there is no seek-back.\n\n## Divergence 4 — `printLine`/`printIntLine` in `lib.rs` use a private, eagerly-flushed buffer\n\n(a) A C consumer `dlopen`s the `.so` and does `printf(\"A-from-caller\\n\"); printLine(\"B-from-lib\"); printf(\"C-from-caller\\n\"); printIntLine(42); printf(\"D-from-caller\\n\");`\n(b) **C `.so`:** `A-from-caller / B-from-lib / C-from-caller / 42 / D-from-caller` (one shared glibc buffer).\n(c) **Rust `.so`:** `B-from-lib / 42 / A-from-caller / C-from-caller / D-from-caller`.\n(d) `lib.rs:32-34` and `lib.rs:40-42`: each wrapper builds a fresh `Io` (`imp.rs:48`, a private `BufWriter`) and calls `io.flush()`, whereas C's `printLine` is just `printf` into the caller's own `FILE *stdout` buffer and never flushes.\n\nSame root cause, second observable: consumer does `printf(\"A-from-caller\\n\"); printLine(\"B-from-lib\"); bad();` with stdin `\"16\\n\"` (fatal index) — **C `.so`:** rc 139, stdout **empty** (both lines lost with the unflushed buffer). **Rust `.so`:** rc 139, stdout **`B-from-lib\\n`**.\n\n## Divergence 5 — exported `bad`/`good` do not share glibc's `stdin`\n\n(a) A C consumer does `fgets(b, 8, stdin)` itself, then calls `good(); bad();`. Stdin is a regular file containing `1\\n2\\n3\\n`.\n(b) **C `.so`:** `caller-read=[1] / <goodG2B: 1 at idx 7> / <goodB2G: data=2, 1 at idx 2> / <bad: data=3, 1 at idx 3>`.\n(c) **Rust `.so`:** `<goodG2B: 1 at idx 7> / fgets() failed. / ERROR: Array index is out-of-bounds / fgets() failed. / ERROR: Array index is negative. / caller-read=[1]` — all three remaining lines are lost, because glibc's `fgets` already pulled the entire file into *its* buffer and Rust's `io::stdin()` sees EOF.\n(d) `imp.rs:40` + `imp.rs:57-67` (`self.stdin.read(...)` on Rust's own `io::Stdin`), reached from `lib.rs:48-50` and `lib.rs:56-58`. Also reproduced with the longer read `abcdefghij\\n7\\n3\\n`.\n\n## Divergence 6 — `FATAL_INDICES` is hard-wired to the *executable's* frame layout, so the exported `bad`/`main` diverge at indices ≤ 200\n\nThe comment block at `imp.rs:170-213` derives the fatal set from the `__libc_start_call_main → main → bad` chain. `lib.rs` exports `bad` and `main` for arbitrary callers, where the frames above are different. Measured through a C consumer that `dlopen`s ea