{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01PzrpDVqZsjfygFqGE6rFNH","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t//! `extern \"C\"` export layer for the translation of `c_src/src/main.c`.\n2\t//!\n3\t//! The C translation unit gives external linkage to `printLine`,\n4\t//! `printIntLine`, `bad`, `good` and `main` (`goodG2B` and `goodB2G` are\n5\t//! `static`, so they are deliberately *not* exported here either). Building\n6\t//! `main.c` with `gcc -shared -fPIC` therefore yields exactly those five\n7\t//! dynamic symbols, and this cdylib exports the same five under the same names\n8\t//! so an external caller cannot tell the two shared objects apart.\n9\t//!\n10\t//! Note what these wrappers do *not* do: they do not flush. The C functions are\n11\t//! plain `printf` calls into the process's `stdout` FILE, leaving the output\n12\t//! buffered for the caller to flush (or to lose, if `bad()`'s out-of-bounds\n13\t//! write kills the process first). Because `imp` writes through that very same\n14\t//! FILE object, doing nothing here is what makes the two libraries\n15\t//! indistinguishable.\n16\t\n17\tmod imp;\n18\t\n19\tuse imp::{Caller, Io};\n20\tuse std::os::raw::{c_char, c_int};\n21\t\n22\t/// `void printLine(const char * line)`\n23\t///\n24\t/// Reproduces the `line != NULL` guard: a NULL argument prints nothing at all.\n25\t#[no_mangle]\n26\tpub extern \"C\" fn printLine(line: *const c_char) {\n27\t    if line.is_null() {\n28\t        return;\n29\t    }\n30\t    Io::new().print_cstr(line);\n31\t}\n32\t\n33\t/// `void printIntLine(int intNumber)`\n34\t#[no_mangle]\n35\tpub extern \"C\" fn printIntLine(int_number: c_int) {\n36\t    Io::new().print_int_line(int_number);\n37\t}\n38\t\n39\t/// `void bad()`\n40\t///\n41\t/// `Caller::Unknown`: the frame above this one belongs to whoever loaded the\n42\t/// shared object, so the out-of-bounds store's effect beyond index 19 is a\n43\t/// property of that caller and cannot be modelled from in here. See the comment\n44\t/// block in `imp.rs`.\n45\t#[no_mangle]\n46\tpub extern \"C\" fn bad() {\n47\t    let mut io = Io::new();\n48\t    // Indices 16..17 clobber this function's own saved frame pointer, so in C the\n49\t    // *consumer* faults as soon as it uses `rbp` again -- after `bad` has returned,\n50\t    // and before it can flush anything it had buffered. Dying as the wrapper\n51\t    // returns is the faithful stand-in: the consumer stops here either way.\n52\t    if imp::bad(&mut io, Caller::Unknown) == imp::Death::AtMainReturn {\n53\t        imp::die_like_c_oob_write();\n54\t    }\n55\t}\n56\t\n57\t/// `void good()`\n58\t#[no_mangle]\n59\tpub extern \"C\" fn good() {\n60\t    let mut io = Io::new();\n61\t    imp::good(&mut io);\n62\t}\n63\t\n64\t/// `int main(int argc, char * argv[])`\n65\t///\n66\t/// `main` has external linkage in the C translation unit and so appears in the\n67\t/// shared object's dynamic symbol table; it is exported here for parity. `argc`\n68\t/// and `argv` are unused by the C body.\n69\t///\n70\t/// `Caller::CMain` is correct here even though this is the library: within this\n71\t/// call `bad()`'s caller really is this `main`, laid out as gcc lays it out, so\n72\t/// indices 26..27 do hit its return address.\n73\t#[no_mangle]\n74\tpub extern \"C\" fn main(_argc: c_int, _argv: *const *const c_char) -> c_int {\n75\t    let mut io = Io::new();\n76\t    imp::run_main(&mut io, Caller::CMain)\n77\t}\n78\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_018tXrCniZHFwoX4NqYTaYXh","session_id":"6de6788a-c8a8-4554-8fe2-4313825db3b2","uuid":"b4859d60-a9a3-4da4-adba-e10864744bb2","timestamp":"2026-08-18T00:55:21.472Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Second audit of rewritten impl"}
