{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_ntniow5qipb27acjx4mon4zfw54rpeola6b2n6hccnrtd273m4na","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification is complete. Final summary:\n\n## What this was\n\n`c_src/src/main.c` is a 155-line Juliet CWE-129 test case (unchecked array index) built by CMake as an **executable**. Cargo.toml had **no `[features]`**, the C has **no `#ifdef`**, and CMakeLists has **no options** — so there is exactly **one** build configuration, mechanically confirmed rather than assumed.\n\nTo do symbol-level differential testing I built the C as a shared object too (it has 5 external functions) and added a `cdylib` to the Rust crate. Tests reach Rust **only** through `libdriver.so` via `libloading` — `crate-type = [\"cdylib\"]` makes direct linking structurally impossible.\n\n## Result: 82 tests, 0 failures, stable across 4 full runs\n\n| gate | status |\n|---|---|\n| `SYMBOLS.md` — 0 missing symbols | 5/5 exported, both `.so`s load under `RTLD_NOW`, Rust needs only system libs |\n| `ERRORS.md` — every row tested | 30/30 rows |\n| `CONFIGS.md` — every row passes randomized inputs | 48/48 rows |\n| Every feature combination | 1/1 (the only one) |\n\n## 11 real divergences found and fixed\n\nThe translation was already correct on all *well-defined* behavior. The bugs were in behavior nobody thinks to test:\n\n1. **The out-of-bounds write was silently skipped.** I disassembled `bad()` and mapped its exact gcc `-O0` frame, so indices 16–19/26–27 now reproduce C's SIGSEGV with the buffered output lost.\n2. **Crash *timing* was wrong** — in C the store doesn't fault, a later `ret` does. C emits 167/151/121 bytes on a tty depending on *which* return address was clobbered; Rust emitted 0.\n3. **SIGPIPE** — C dies with signal 13; Rust's runtime sets `SIG_IGN`, so it exited 0.\n4. **Seekable stdin** — glibc rewinds it at exit so `{ driver; cat; } < file` sees the remainder; Rust's `BufReader` consumed the file.\n5–7. **Shared `FILE` state** — a C consumer's `printf`/`fgets` must interleave and cooperate with the library's.\n8. **The fatal index set was hardwired** to the executable's call chain, so the exported `bad()` crashed where C didn't.\n\nDivergences 2–7 all had one root cause: `std::io` is not C stdio. I rewrote the I/O layer onto the same glibc `stdin`/`printf`/`fgets` the C uses, which fixed all six at once.\n\nTwo judgment calls worth flagging:\n- **I did not fabricate faults.** Indices 16/17 kill a `gcc -O0` caller but not an optimized one — I observed *both* with the same C `.so`. So the export models only the caller-independent case (18/19) and an explicit `Caller` enum handles the known-`main` case.\n- **I fixed a doc error in my own artifact**: I'd written that `atoi(\"-999999999999\")` stays negative. It truncates to **+727379969** — the sign is not preserved. Now asserted in code so the claim is machine-checked.\n\n## One region is irreducibly unmatchable\n\nBroad fuzz went from **37/940 → 6/940** divergences. All 6 survivors are `bad()` indices ~3600–4600, and they are **not Rust bugs — C disagrees with itself**:\n\n| input | C (30 runs) | Rust (30 runs) |\n|---|---|---|\n| `12345678901234567\\n` | 20 crash / 10 ok | 19 crash / 11 ok |\n| `-202740845\\n3737\\n` | 4 / 26 | 4 / 26 |\n| `-7\\n3848\\n` | 12 / 18 | 11 / 19 |\n\nStack ASLR decides whether the write lands past the stack, so single-shot comparison catches both sides flipping the same coin. `imp.rs` reads the live `[stack]` bounds from `/proc/self/maps` so the boundary tracks the environment; tests assert exactly *outside* the band with a **runtime-probed** limit (hardcoding it would be wrong — with an empty environment even index 500 sometimes faults), and `oob_band.rs` asserts the statistical envelope so deleting the emulation would still fail.\n\nTwo related cases are documented the same way: which fatal signal a far write raises (`SIGBUS` vs `SIGSEGV`, also ASLR-dependent), and consumer-frame indices through the `.so`.\n\nIndependent confirmation beyond the suite: exhaustive sweep of indices −8..300 × both sinks × 3 runs (**0** divergences, **0** nondeterminism), 3200-case fuzz (1 band flip), 200 tty cases (0), 560 NUL-position/all-byte-value cases (0), 100 KB + format-specifier + non-UTF-8 output byte-identical by md5, and no stderr output from either side in any case.\n\nI also had to fix `verify.sh` itself: it printed **PASS** when `/tmp` turned out to be read-only. It now honors `TMPDIR`, probes writability, and fails on \"0 tests run\" — a green check that can't fail is worse than no check."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":985,"cache_read_input_tokens":286354,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":985,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6de6788a-c8a8-4554-8fe2-4313825db3b2","uuid":"c9b4b072-151a-4b06-a656-8759ad21327e","timestamp":"2026-08-18T01:17:06.755Z"}
